Integer minor units represent money as a whole integer count of a currency's smallest subdivision (such as cents, kobo, or fils) rather than a decimal or floating-point number. For example, $19.99 is stored or transmitted as 1999 with "USD", and ₦450.00 is 45000 with "NGN". [1] (https://cardinalby.github.io/blog/post/best-practices/storing-currency-values-data-types/), [2] (https://docs.reevit.io/amounts), [3] (https://medium.com/@martin128/money-is-not-a-float-storing-amounts-in-node-js-and-postgres-9142bb8c5714), [4] (https://www.syntaqx.com/docs/money/amounts)

Why Systems Use Integer Minor Units

• Exact arithmetic: Whole-number addition, subtraction, and equality checks avoid binary floating-point rounding errors (such as 0.1 + 0.2 drifting).
• API & payment standards: Many payment processors and APIs expect amounts as integers in minor units, as explained in guides on storing currency values and value objects for money and units.
• Explicit scale: You convert to major display units (dividing by \(10^{\text{exponent}}\)) only at display boundaries like UIs, receipts, or exports. [1] (https://docs.adyen.com/development-resources/currency-codes), [2] (https://boldtechlabs.com/insights/storing-money-as-minor-units), [3] (https://www.youtube.com/shorts/J_iTz2C1c-Y), [4] (https://www.skillsdirectory.com/skills/zakariaf-value-objects-money-and-units), [5] (https://www.syntaqx.com/docs/money/amounts), [6] (https://medium.com/@martin128/money-is-not-a-float-storing-amounts-in-node-js-and-postgres-9142bb8c5714), [7] (https://cardinalby.github.io/blog/post/best-practices/storing-currency-values-data-types/)

Common Currency Exponents

Not every currency uses two decimal places: [1] (https://subtracker.io/build/multi-currency-money-storage), [2] (https://medium.com/@martin128/money-is-not-a-float-storing-amounts-in-node-js-and-postgres-9142bb8c5714)
• 0 decimals (factor of 1): JPY (1500 = ¥1,500), XOF (45000 = XOF 45,000)
• 2 decimals (factor of 100): USD (1999 = $19.99), NGN (45000 = ₦450.00), GBP (1000 = £10.00)
• 3 decimals (factor of 1,000): KWD (1250 = KWD 1.250), BHD (10000 = BHD 10.000) [1] (https://www.syntaqx.com/docs/money/amounts), [2] (https://docs.adyen.com/development-resources/currency-codes), [3] (https://docs.reevit.io/amounts), [4] (https://medium.com/@martin128/money-is-not-a-float-storing-amounts-in-node-js-and-postgres-9142bb8c5714)

Key Pitfalls & Trade-offs

• 64-bit integer limits: Use BIGINT / 64-bit integers in databases and code rather than 32-bit integers, which can overflow on large totals.
• Provider quirks: Some payment gateways diverge from standard ISO 4217 minor-unit exponents for specific currencies, so keep a per-integration exponent mapping.
• FX & interest math: Opinions on storing money values as cents/minor units are mixed on fractional calculations—while integer minor units prevent float drift, exchange rates, interest, and percentages still need high-precision decimal math and clear rounding rules. [1] (https://stackoverflow.com/questions/18934774/what-are-the-downsides-to-storing-money-values-as-cents-minor-units), [2] (https://www.syntaqx.com/docs/money/amounts)
