UTC (Coordinated Universal Time) is the single global reference clock, whereas a time zone is a local rule that offsets from UTC for a specific region and accounts for Daylight Saving Time (DST).

Key Differences


Feature	UTC	Time Zone
Role	The global baseline standard for time.	A regional rule for local wall-clock time.
Offset	Always +00:00 (never shifts).	Fixed or changing offset from UTC (e.g., UTC+01:00 or UTC-05:00).
Daylight Saving Time	Does not observe DST.	May shift forward or backward during the year.
Examples	2026-11-19T00:39:00Z	Africa/Lagos (WAT, UTC+1), America/New_York (EST/EDT)


How They Work Together

• Calculating local time: Local time equals UTC plus the time zone's current offset. For example, 00:00 UTC is 01:00 WAT in Lagos (UTC+1), and 19:00 EST the previous evening in New York (UTC-5).
• Software & databases: Systems store timestamps in UTC (or Unix epoch time) so events stay in a single, unambiguous order across regions, then convert them to a user's time zone only for display.
• Future scheduling: Scheduling future local events (like a 9:00 AM meeting next summer) requires saving the named IANA time zone (such as Europe/London) alongside the local wall time, because governments can change DST rules or offsets.
