Recording a time zone (or offset) alongside a timestamp preserves local human context and prevents scheduling bugs that UTC alone cannot solve. While UTC tells you when an instant happened globally, the time zone tells you what the local clock read where it happened or where it will happen.

1. Future Scheduled Events (The Biggest Reason)

• Governments change rules: Countries frequently add, cancel, or shift Daylight Saving Time (DST) dates.
• Preserving human intent: If a user schedules a daily alarm or meeting for 9:00 AM in New York, storing only a fixed UTC timestamp breaks when DST starts or ends—the meeting would suddenly jump to 8:00 AM or 10:00 AM local time.
• Named time zones solve this: Saving "09:00" + "America/New_York" lets software recalculate the exact UTC instant using the latest regional rules.

2. Local Business Context & Reporting

• End-of-day cutoffs: A card transaction at 01:30 UTC on November 19 occurred at 02:30 AM on November 19 in Lagos (UTC+1), but at 8:30 PM on November 18 in New York (UTC-5).
• Analytics and compliance: Accounting ledgers, store shift logs, fraud detection ("was this purchase made at 3:00 AM local time?"), and tax reporting all depend on the local calendar date and hour where the event took place.

3. Avoiding Ambiguity in Recorded Strings

• Bare local times are ambiguous: Writing "2026-11-19 01:44:00" without a time zone or Z makes it impossible to know which part of the world it came from.
• DST fall-back collisions: When clocks turn back one hour in autumn, local times like 01:30 AM happen twice on the same night—only an offset or time zone distinguishes the first occurrence from the second.

Best Practice Summary

• Past events & system logs: Record in UTC (or ISO 8601 with offset, like 2026-11-19T01:44:00+01:00), and store the origin time zone separately if local reporting matters.
• Future user-facing schedules: Store the local wall time plus the IANA time zone ID (e.g., Africa/Lagos).
