This code-review feedback points out a common validation mistake: applying strict write/recording rules to read/search queries. Rejecting any search filter whose end date is in the future breaks standard reporting workflows like "this month" or "this quarter."

1. The Core Problem in Your Validation

• What your rule currently does: It treats any end_date > today in a search request as an invalid input error (400 Bad Request / ValueError).
• Why it breaks real usage: On September 15, a user or dashboard generating a "September" report naturally passes start_date = Sept 1 and end_date = Sept 30. Because Sept 30 > Sept 15, your validator rejects a completely valid query instead of returning the data recorded between Sept 1 and Sept 15.

2. Breaking Down Hint 1

• "What is the natural end date of a calendar-month range?"
	• The natural end of September is September 30 (or October 1 00:00:00 as an exclusive upper bound).
	• Reporting tools, date pickers, and preset buttons ("This Month", "This Year", "Q3") calculate the full period boundary, not "today."
• "Is a filter the same kind of thing as recording a transaction?"
	• No. When recording a transaction (a write operation), a future timestamp usually means a bug, clock skew, or fraudulent entry, so rejecting timestamp > now protects data integrity.
	• When filtering/searching (a read operation), a date range is simply a query window (WHERE created_at >= start AND created_at <= end). If no transactions exist between September 16 and September 30 yet, the database naturally returns zero rows for those days without any harm.

3. How to Revise (or Defend) the Rule

• Recommended revision (Allow future end_date in searches):
	• Keep start_date <= end_date: Reject inverted ranges where the start comes after the end (e.g., Sept 30 to Sept 1).
	• Drop the end_date <= today check on search filters: Let queries span into the future so "This Month", "This Year", and custom future-bounded windows work seamlessly.
	• (Optional) If you still want to catch nonsensical queries, you can either clamp effective_end = min(end_date, now) internally or only block absurdly far-future dates (e.g., > 10 years ahead) if performance is a concern.
• When you could defend keeping it (rare):
	• Only if your API contract explicitly defines a specialized endpoint for "completed historical days only" where partial-period data would mislead downstream consumers—though even then, a warning flag or clamping is usually friendlier than throwing an error.
