• Single implementation: All chronological balance checks live in one function in services/:
validate_account_timeline(account_id: str, transactions: list[Transaction]) -> None
• How it works:
	1. Filters transactions to all entries where source_acc_id == account_id or target_acc_id == account_id.
	2. Sorts those entries chronologically by (transaction_date, insertion_order).
	3. Walks through the sorted list maintaining running_balance (starting at 0), adding trx.amount when target_acc_id == account_id and subtracting trx.amount when source_acc_id == account_id.
	4. If running_balance < 0 at any point in the timeline, raises NegativeBalanceError(account_id, trx.transaction_date, running_balance).
• How TRANSFER (source = A, target = B) is validated:
	• services/ constructs proposed_transactions = [*self._data["transactions"], transfer_trx] and validates both endpoints through the exact same function:
		• validate_account_timeline(source_acc_id, proposed_transactions)
		• validate_account_timeline(target_acc_id, proposed_transactions)
	• Running both accounts through the same timeline function guarantees both timelines remain valid under one rule; if source_acc_id dips below 0 on transaction_date (or any subsequent date), NegativeBalanceError aborts the transfer atomically before persistence.

3. What the User Sees on a Rejected Write (NegativeBalanceError vs. InsufficientFundsError)

• Decision: Retire InsufficientFundsError in BR-10 and standardize on NegativeBalanceError(account_id, date, balance) across both startup validation and write operations.
• Why NegativeBalanceError is better for users here:
	• Because our app allows backdated transactions, a write can be rejected even when today's balance looks positive (e.g., recording a ₦5,000 expense dated Nov 10 when Nov 10's balance was ₦0, even though ₦10,000 income arrived on Nov 15). Saying "insufficient funds" when the user sees ₦10,000 in their current balance is confusing.
