2. Reasoning About Intra-Day Debit Order (The Simpler Statement of the Rule)

• Can the order among debits within the same day ever change whether that day passes?
	• No, never. Here is why:
		• Within a single day \(D\), once all credits (\(C_1, C_2, \dots\)) are applied first, the balance reaches its daily peak:
\(\text{Peak}_{D}=\text{StartBalance}_{D}+\sum \text{Credits}_{D}\)
		• Every subsequent debit (\(d_1, d_2, \dots\)) only subtracts a positive amount (amount > 0), so the running balance decreases monotonically across the debits until it reaches its lowest point of the day after the final debit:
\(\text{EndBalance}_{D}=\text{Peak}_{D}-\sum \text{Debits}_{D}\)
		• Because addition/subtraction is commutative, every permutation of the day's debits lands on the exact same EndBalance_D, and no intermediate debit can ever dip lower than EndBalance_D!
• What the rule actually says & how to state it much more simply:
	• Ordering all same-day credits before all same-day debits is mathematically identical to checking end-of-day net balance:"Group transactions by transaction_date (or evaluate net daily delta sum(inflows) - sum(outflows) per date in chronological date order) and verify that running_balance >= 0 at the end of every calendar day."
